speechtext:
The minister said a few moments ago that one of the reasons why cooperatives could not be considered as eligible under section 9 of the National Housing Act was that they were cooperatives only with respect to building houses. If they were cooperating on something else; if they were a cooperative that entered into some other kind of business, they would be eligible. I just want to point out that the cooperative organization of which I spoke a few moments ago, the York Mills Cooperative Home Builders, give the following as their objects and general programme;
The objects of the York Mills Cooperative Home Builders shall be as stated in the letter patent, viz.: cooperatively for the members,-
(a)	To promote home ownership, and to buy, lease, sell, mortgage, exchange, administer, and otherwise deal in real and personal property, to deal in building of homes or other buildings required in connection with homes, and in the creation of parks, playgrounds, swimming pools and the like, with all equipment and accessories in connection therewith; and,
(b)	to carry on in all its branches the business of landscape architects and landscape artists and in connection with such business to deal in. buy and sell flowers, shrubs, and every kind of plant and tree;
(c)	to do all such other things as are incidental or conducive to the attainment of the above objects;
(d)	to draw, make, exchange, endorse, discount, execute and issue promissory notes, bills of exchange, bills of lading, warrants, and other negotiable or transferable instruments.
That is only part of their programme; it is more than merely building houses, and it seems to me that should bring this organization right within the minister's own definition, as one that should receive the benefits of such an act.
